    "rationale": "Measured evidence: the clean baseline median latency is 4.2250 ms (project.json original_baseline) while the 88 associated CUDA kernels of one forward sum to 4152.3 us (call-resources.json, root call), so the workload is GPU-bound with only ~73 us of host/launch headroom; host-side plays (CUDA graphs, Python trimming) and their binding/coverage hazards are therefore rejected. Kernel composition inside the trace: conv math 2978.7 us (71.7%, frozen: FP32, TF32 off, cudnn.benchmark off, Winograd/implicit-GEMM ampere kernels already selected), but 889.7 us (21.4%) is memory-bound eager pointwise work that is a direct consequence of the transformers 5.16.1 structure in the selected region (modeling_resnet.py:65-69,85-93,130-136): every conv is followed by a separate cudnn bn_fw_inf_1C11_kernel_NCHW pass (425.4 us x20), a separate clamp-based ReLU pass (343.9 us x17), and each of the 8 residual blocks adds a separate CUDAFunctor_add pass plus another ReLU pass (120.4 us x8). Per block that is up to 5 full-tensor passes (bn1, relu1, bn2, add, relu2) for chains that evaluate to one elementwise expression, y = relu(bn_affine(conv2(x)) + shortcut(x)); at ~1.4 TB/s effective clamp-kernel bandwidth, collapsing bn+relu to one pass and bn2+add+relu to one pass removes roughly half or more of that traffic. The stem (ResNetEmbeddings, 908.5 us incl. the 265.1 us 7x7 conv and 198.2 us max_pool_forward_nchw running at only ~770 GB/s effective) and all 8 ResNetBasicLayer invocations form one coherent editable region covering 87.5% of diagnostic CUDA time (the 3631.9 us conv-layer family nests entirely inside); the parent wrappers (stages/encoder/model) and the ~13 us pooler+classifier head stay outside and untouched. The edits are interdependent: conv2 normalization must fuse with the block-level add+relu, and the stem activation with the stem normalization, so this is one region rather than independent group edits; module-level boundaries ResNetEmbeddings + the 4 Re